#{[17592186524299 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i-k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+2j \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i-4j-2k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{41} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{205}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524306 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+2k)-2k \\\\  & =2i-3j \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-2k \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =-3i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{113} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1469}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524307 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+2k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i-3j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{139} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1390}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524309 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-6i+2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{89} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524312 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j+3k)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i+6j-3k \\\\  & =⟨9,6,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{126} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{14} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{182}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524316 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-7k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ 7 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{10}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186524999 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite"] [17592186524999 :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186525014 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite"] [17592186525014 :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6,\\,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186525064 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186525217 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525227 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525334 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525396 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525422 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525461 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525491 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525502 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525564 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525607 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525680 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525687 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525720 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525722 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525815 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186525984 :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\}$ is orthonormal"] [17592186525984 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=2+2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186525985 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{30} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n{{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [17592186525985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{(-1)}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186526003 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the fourth principal component of the data?"] [17592186526041 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?"] [17592186526056 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?"] [17592186526056 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186526064 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the first principal component of the data?"] [17592186526064 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186527136 :question/problem "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$"] [17592186527136 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x}{y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{2x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right)-x\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3v}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186527148 :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$"] [17592186527148 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"] [17592186527961 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$"] [17592186527961 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}=\\underbrace{(\\,6\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"] [17592186528313 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2} & {u}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186528611 :answer/value "{X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186528643 :answer/value "{X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186528694 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186528709 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2} & {u}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186528710 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186529087 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ under this inner product."] [17592186529096 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.56 & -0.17 & 0.71 & -0.393 \\\\ 0.682 & -0.53 & 0.486 & 0.135 \\\\ 0.264 & 0.821 & 0.485 & 0.144 \\\\ -0.39 & -0.126 & 0.159 & 0.898\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.085 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.292 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 & 0.708 \\\\ 0.708 & -0.707\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.98 & -0.67\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186529104 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2.389\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2.389\n$$"] [17592186529104 :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=U\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186529109 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186529111 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.29 & 0.813 & 0.505 & 0.021 \\\\ -0.469 & -0.212 & 0.634 & -0.578 \\\\ 0.676 & -0.54 & 0.473 & 0.168 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.061 & 0.345 & 0.798\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.065 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.316 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 & -0.705 \\\\ 0.705 & 0.709\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2.065\n$$"] [17592186529111 :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=U\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186529115 :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$"] [17592186529116 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186529129 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.866 & 1.212\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186529136 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 1.917\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186529136 :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=U\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186529137 :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ under this inner product."] [17592186529155 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186529168 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186529174 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2.506 & 4.309 & 6.738 \\\\ 0.552 & 4.174 & 1.558 \\\\ 0.175 & 3.635 & 2.487 \\\\ 1.127 & 4.541 & 5.878 \\\\ 4.088 & 4.508 & 5.688\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$"] [17592186529174 :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈58.0\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈92.6\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $58.0\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $92.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."] [17592186529209 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -1 & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 2 & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}- \\sqrt{2})+1(- \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+4 \\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{20}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(Q)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$Q$ is a singular matrix” and $det(Q)=0$ are true."] [17592186529214 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{vmatrix}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{9}{4})+(- \\sqrt{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}+\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{5} \\\\  & =1- \\frac{7}{10}- \\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{13}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(R)=- \\frac{13}{10}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$R$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."] [17592186529220 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5})+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)(- \\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}(\\frac{12 \\sqrt{2}}{25}- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25}) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{2}{5}\\right)\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{6 \\sqrt{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{3}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=\\frac{6}{25}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$D$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."] [17592186529229 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}- \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & 1 & - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{7}}{5}